<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  RELAX NG schema for the Vitrin protocol IDL dialect (vitrin-v0.xml).

  The dialect is a stricter subset of the Wayland protocol XML shape, plus
  exactly two extension attributes: protocol/@version (the single source of
  truth for the hello version integer) and interface/@verb (declares that
  every request on the interface exercises the named grant verb; the scanner
  derives the enforcement chokepoint's opcode-to-verb table from it).

  interface/@verb is a closed value set, and widening it is a DIALECT change
  that moves with the IDL: a verb name the schema does not admit is rejected
  here rather than reaching a backend that would emit an unenforceable
  chokepoint entry. The set below tracks the facet-bearing verbs of
  vitrin_grant.verb, and is therefore SHORTER than that bitfield.

  vitrin-verb-set: facetless-verbs = observe_cursor

  ONE verb is missing from it, and it belongs here because a reader meeting
  a list shorter than the bitfield will ask why: observe_cursor has no facet
  interface BY CONSTRUCTION. It widens what capture_frame composites rather
  than adding a request, so there is no interface for the attribute to sit
  on and there never will be.

  It was TWO until P2.7.2's second half. egress had no facet YET - the task
  landed the verb bit with no message at all, and this comment said that its
  facet, when it landed, would be an interface of its own rather than a
  request on the filesystem powerbox, and that this value set would gain
  "egress" in that same commit. Both happened: vitrin_egress is that
  interface and "egress" is below. The prediction is kept here rather than
  deleted, because the rule it turned on - @verb is one value per interface,
  so an interface declaring verb="designate_file" cannot also declare
  verb="egress" - is the same rule that split the layout facet, and it will
  be the same rule the next time. designate_file never entered this count,
  because it never spent a moment facetless: P2.6.5 landed the bit and
  vitrin_powerbox in one change, which is the shape P2.7.2 could not take
  because its bit had already shipped a task ahead of its facet.

  observe_cursor is also what protocol/test-mutations.sh's
  verb-without-facet case draws on: it mutates a legal @verb into a real
  grant verb the set does not admit, and only a facetless verb can play that
  part. Pinning the case on the BY-CONSTRUCTION one is deliberate - the case
  was pinned on layout_arrange until that verb gained an interface, at which
  point the mutated document became legal and the case reported the schema
  as broken, and egress would have done the same thing here.

  The marker line above, and its sibling on the attribute itself further
  down, are read by `cargo xtask verb-sets`. That tool derives both sets from
  protocol/vitrin-v0.xml, holds the value set below to the facet-bearing one,
  and fails when this comment (or any other surface enumerating either set)
  falls behind. xmllint sees only one direction of that pairing - an IDL
  using a name this schema omits - and can never see a schema admitting a
  name no interface declares.

  layout_arrange, layout_focus and egress were in that no-facet list until
  they gained one each (vitrin_layout_arrange, vitrin_layout_focus,
  vitrin_egress). The layout pair are two interfaces rather than one, and
  that is forced by this attribute rather than chosen: @verb is one value
  per interface, so a single combined layout facet could name only one verb
  and the other would reach the chokepoint with no entry. D-018(3) requires
  the two to be independently attenuable, and one attribute per interface is
  how the dialect makes that structural. The same rule split the powerbox:
  egress cannot be a second verb on the interface that already declares
  designate_file, so vitrin_egress is its own interface and vitrin_powerbox
  will carry the filesystem half alone.

  A verb having a facet does not make it SERVED. egress has an interface
  here and no deployment serves the verb, because the mediating proxy a
  connection would be made through does not exist. This schema's value set
  is about which verbs have a request to be exercised through, never about
  which deployments answer.

  designate_file joined the set with vitrin_powerbox (P2.6.5). The same
  one-value-per-interface rule that split the layout pair decided the NEXT
  verb through that facet before it was written:
  docs/plan/02-phase-2-semantic-epochs.md E2.7/P2.7.2 was drafted as
  request_connect on vitrin_powerbox exercising egress, which this attribute
  cannot express on an interface already declaring designate_file. Egress
  therefore takes a second interface, as the layout pair did; this closed
  set grows by one value when it lands, and no interface ever carries two.

  Structural rules this schema enforces beyond wayland.dtd:
    - argument types are a closed set of seven (no array);
    - new_id and object arguments MUST name their interface (no untyped
      new_id; codegen stays a straight-line table);
    - allow-null is legal only on string and object arguments;
    - enum references are legal only on int and uint arguments;
    - descriptions are required on the protocol, every interface, and every
      request, event, and enum; every enum entry and every argument carries
      a summary; string-argument summaries carry the machine-readable
      "(max N bytes)" bound token;
    - enum entry values are required (decimal or hex);
    - B2: every vitrin_shim_seat event's final argument is the origin tag
      (and the seat interface defines no requests).

  Validate with: xmllint - -relaxng protocol/vitrin-v0.rng protocol/vitrin-v0.xml
  (without the space between the dashes).
-->
<grammar xmlns="http://relaxng.org/ns/structure/1.0"
         datatypeLibrary="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-datatypes">

  <start>
    <ref name="protocol"/>
  </start>

  <define name="protocol">
    <element name="protocol">
      <attribute name="name">
        <data type="NCName"/>
      </attribute>
      <attribute name="version">
        <data type="positiveInteger"/>
      </attribute>
      <optional>
        <element name="copyright">
          <text/>
        </element>
      </optional>
      <ref name="description"/>
      <oneOrMore>
        <ref name="interface"/>
      </oneOrMore>
    </element>
  </define>

  <define name="description">
    <element name="description">
      <attribute name="summary">
        <text/>
      </attribute>
      <text/>
    </element>
  </define>

  <define name="interface">
    <choice>
      <ref name="seat-interface"/>
      <ref name="generic-interface"/>
    </choice>
  </define>

  <!-- Every interface except vitrin_shim_seat. -->
  <define name="generic-interface">
    <element name="interface">
      <attribute name="name">
        <data type="NCName">
          <except>
            <value type="NCName">vitrin_shim_seat</value>
          </except>
        </data>
      </attribute>
      <ref name="interface-common-attrs"/>
      <ref name="description"/>
      <zeroOrMore>
        <choice>
          <ref name="request"/>
          <ref name="event"/>
          <ref name="enum"/>
        </choice>
      </zeroOrMore>
    </element>
  </define>

  <!--
    vitrin_shim_seat, specialized: events only, and every event's final
    argument is the origin tag (backward requirement B2, enforced by the
    schema rather than by convention).
  -->
  <define name="seat-interface">
    <element name="interface">
      <attribute name="name">
        <value type="NCName">vitrin_shim_seat</value>
      </attribute>
      <ref name="interface-common-attrs"/>
      <ref name="description"/>
      <zeroOrMore>
        <choice>
          <ref name="seat-event"/>
          <ref name="enum"/>
        </choice>
      </zeroOrMore>
    </element>
  </define>

  <define name="interface-common-attrs">
    <attribute name="version">
      <data type="positiveInteger"/>
    </attribute>
    <optional>
      <attribute name="verb">
        <!-- vitrin-verb-set: facet-verbs = observe, actuate_pointer, actuate_text, layout_arrange, layout_focus, designate_file, egress, realm_launch -->
        <choice>
          <value>observe</value>
          <value>actuate_pointer</value>
          <value>actuate_text</value>
          <value>realm_launch</value>
          <value>layout_arrange</value>
          <value>layout_focus</value>
          <value>designate_file</value>
          <value>egress</value>
        </choice>
      </attribute>
    </optional>
  </define>

  <define name="message-common-attrs">
    <attribute name="name">
      <data type="NCName"/>
    </attribute>
    <optional>
      <attribute name="type">
        <value>destructor</value>
      </attribute>
    </optional>
    <optional>
      <attribute name="since">
        <data type="positiveInteger"/>
      </attribute>
    </optional>
    <optional>
      <attribute name="deprecated-since">
        <data type="positiveInteger"/>
      </attribute>
    </optional>
  </define>

  <define name="request">
    <element name="request">
      <ref name="message-common-attrs"/>
      <ref name="description"/>
      <zeroOrMore>
        <ref name="arg"/>
      </zeroOrMore>
    </element>
  </define>

  <define name="event">
    <element name="event">
      <ref name="message-common-attrs"/>
      <ref name="description"/>
      <zeroOrMore>
        <ref name="arg"/>
      </zeroOrMore>
    </element>
  </define>

  <define name="seat-event">
    <element name="event">
      <ref name="message-common-attrs"/>
      <ref name="description"/>
      <zeroOrMore>
        <ref name="arg"/>
      </zeroOrMore>
      <ref name="origin-arg"/>
    </element>
  </define>

  <define name="origin-arg">
    <element name="arg">
      <attribute name="name">
        <value>origin</value>
      </attribute>
      <attribute name="type">
        <value>uint</value>
      </attribute>
      <attribute name="enum">
        <value>origin</value>
      </attribute>
      <ref name="arg-summary"/>
    </element>
  </define>

  <define name="arg-summary">
    <attribute name="summary">
      <text/>
    </attribute>
  </define>

  <define name="arg">
    <element name="arg">
      <attribute name="name">
        <data type="NCName"/>
      </attribute>
      <choice>
        <!-- new_id: must name its interface; never nullable -->
        <group>
          <attribute name="type">
            <value>new_id</value>
          </attribute>
          <attribute name="interface">
            <data type="NCName"/>
          </attribute>
          <ref name="arg-summary"/>
        </group>
        <!-- object: must name its interface; may be nullable -->
        <group>
          <attribute name="type">
            <value>object</value>
          </attribute>
          <attribute name="interface">
            <data type="NCName"/>
          </attribute>
          <optional>
            <attribute name="allow-null">
              <value>true</value>
            </attribute>
          </optional>
          <ref name="arg-summary"/>
        </group>
        <!-- string: may be nullable; no interface; summary must carry the
             machine-readable "(max N bytes)" bound token -->
        <group>
          <attribute name="type">
            <value>string</value>
          </attribute>
          <optional>
            <attribute name="allow-null">
              <value>true</value>
            </attribute>
          </optional>
          <attribute name="summary">
            <data type="string">
              <param name="pattern">.*\(max [0-9]+ bytes\).*</param>
            </data>
          </attribute>
        </group>
        <!-- scalar: may reference an enum -->
        <group>
          <attribute name="type">
            <choice>
              <value>int</value>
              <value>uint</value>
            </choice>
          </attribute>
          <optional>
            <attribute name="enum">
              <data type="string">
                <param name="pattern">[a-z0-9_]+(\.[a-z0-9_]+)?</param>
              </data>
            </attribute>
          </optional>
          <ref name="arg-summary"/>
        </group>
        <!-- fixed and fd: bare -->
        <group>
          <attribute name="type">
            <choice>
              <value>fixed</value>
              <value>fd</value>
            </choice>
          </attribute>
          <ref name="arg-summary"/>
        </group>
      </choice>
    </element>
  </define>

  <define name="enum">
    <element name="enum">
      <attribute name="name">
        <data type="NCName"/>
      </attribute>
      <optional>
        <attribute name="since">
          <data type="positiveInteger"/>
        </attribute>
      </optional>
      <optional>
        <attribute name="bitfield">
          <choice>
            <value>true</value>
            <value>false</value>
          </choice>
        </attribute>
      </optional>
      <ref name="description"/>
      <oneOrMore>
        <ref name="entry"/>
      </oneOrMore>
    </element>
  </define>

  <define name="entry">
    <element name="entry">
      <attribute name="name">
        <data type="NCName"/>
      </attribute>
      <attribute name="value">
        <data type="string">
          <param name="pattern">(0x[0-9a-fA-F]+|[0-9]+)</param>
        </data>
      </attribute>
      <attribute name="summary">
        <text/>
      </attribute>
      <optional>
        <attribute name="since">
          <data type="positiveInteger"/>
        </attribute>
      </optional>
      <optional>
        <attribute name="deprecated-since">
          <data type="positiveInteger"/>
        </attribute>
      </optional>
    </element>
  </define>

</grammar>
